# AIST Cursor Rules
# These rules guide Cursor AI behaviour for this project

## Project Context
AIST is an AI agent security testing tool written in Python.
It tests target AI agents for prompt injection vulnerabilities.
The tool itself must be secure -- all agent responses are
untrusted input and must never be passed raw to any LLM.

## Language and Style
- Python 3.10+
- Type hints on all functions
- Docstrings on all classes and public methods
- snake_case for variables and functions
- PascalCase for classes
- Maximum line length 88 characters (Black formatter)
- No print statements -- use the logger module only

## Security Rules (non-negotiable)
- Never hardcode API keys, tokens, or credentials
- Always use environment variables via python-dotenv
- Never pass raw agent responses to any LLM component
- Always sanitise and mask sensitive data before logging
- Validate and sanitise all user inputs
- Use httpx not requests for all HTTP calls
- Set timeouts on all outbound HTTP requests
- Never use pickle or eval

## File Structure
- Core package lives in aist/
- Tests mirror package structure in tests/
- All payloads are YAML files in aist/payloads/
- Reports output to reports/ directory
- Logs output to logs/ directory

## Dependencies
- httpx for HTTP requests
- structlog for logging
- click for CLI
- jinja2 for HTML report templating
- pyyaml for payload files
- python-dotenv for environment variables
- rich for terminal output

## Testing
- Every module gets a corresponding test file
- Use pytest
- Test files named test_modulename.py

## Git
- Commit messages start with type:
  feat, fix, docs, security, test, refactor
- Always pull before starting work
- Always push before stopping work
- Never commit .env files or API keys